The strings can be read directly without building arrays. Two indexes move through the versions, and each loop collects the digits until the next dot.

When one index has already reached the end, its current number stays zero. That takes care of versions with different numbers of parts. The input strings are not changed.

A small dry run with `1.0.3` and `1.0.2` gives equal values for the first two parts, then `3` versus `2`, so the result is `1`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int compareVersion(string version1, string version2) {
        int index1 = 0;
        int index2 = 0;
        int length1 = version1.size();
        int length2 = version2.size();

        while (index1 < length1 || index2 < length2) {
            long long number1 = 0;
            long long number2 = 0;

            while (index1 < length1 && version1[index1] != '.') {
                number1 = number1 * 10 + (version1[index1] - '0');
                index1++;
            }

            while (index2 < length2 && version2[index2] != '.') {
                number2 = number2 * 10 + (version2[index2] - '0');
                index2++;
            }

            if (number1 < number2) {
                return -1;
            }
            if (number1 > number2) {
                return 1;
            }

            if (index1 < length1) {
                index1++;
            }
            if (index2 < length2) {
                index2++;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)` because every character is read once.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input strings. This version assumes each revision fits in a `long long`.

A helper reads one revision at a time and returns both its numeric value and the next position. This keeps the main method focused on comparing the two current parts.

The loop continues while either string still has data. A missing part naturally produces zero. Here is a dry run for `1.0` and `1.0.0.1`: the first three compared values are equal, then the missing part from the first version is `0`, which is less than `1`.

```python
class Solution:
    def compareVersion(self, version1: str, version2: str) -> int:
        def read_part(version: str, position: int):
            value = 0

            while position < len(version) and version[position] != '.':
                value = value * 10 + int(version[position])
                position += 1

            if position < len(version):
                position += 1

            return value, position

        position1 = 0
        position2 = 0

        while position1 < len(version1) or position2 < len(version2):
            value1, position1 = read_part(version1, position1)
            value2, position2 = read_part(version2, position2)

            if value1 < value2:
                return -1
            if value1 > value2:
                return 1

        return 0
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`, since the helper visits each character once.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from a few local values. Empty or zero-only revisions compare as zero, and no input string is modified.

Instead of relying on `int`, each dot-separated part is read as a `BigInteger`. This keeps the numeric comparison correct even if a revision has many digits.

The first different revision decides the answer. If one version has no part at a position, `BigInteger.ZERO` is used. For `1.000` and `1`, the second comparison is `0` versus a missing `0`, so the result is equal.

```java
class Solution {
    public int compareVersion(String version1, String version2) {
        String[] parts1 = version1.split("\\.");
        String[] parts2 = version2.split("\\.");
        int length = Math.max(parts1.length, parts2.length);

        for (int index = 0; index < length; index++) {
            java.math.BigInteger value1 = index < parts1.length
                    ? new java.math.BigInteger(parts1[index])
                    : java.math.BigInteger.ZERO;
            java.math.BigInteger value2 = index < parts2.length
                    ? new java.math.BigInteger(parts2[index])
                    : java.math.BigInteger.ZERO;

            int comparison = value1.compareTo(value2);
            if (comparison < 0) {
                return -1;
            }
            if (comparison > 0) {
                return 1;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)` digit processing, with the cost of comparing the revision values.
Space complexity: `O(n + m)` for the split parts and `BigInteger` objects. This uses more memory than direct parsing, but it avoids numeric overflow.

This version avoids converting a revision to an integer. For each part, it removes leading zeroes, then compares the remaining lengths and digits.

That is useful when a revision could be too large for normal numeric types. An empty result after removing zeroes means the revision is zero. Missing parts are also treated as zero.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public int CompareVersion(string version1, string version2) {
        string[] parts1 = version1.Split('.');
        string[] parts2 = version2.Split('.');
        int count = Math.Max(parts1.Length, parts2.Length);

        for (int index = 0; index < count; index++) {
            string part1 = index < parts1.Length ? RemoveLeadingZeroes(parts1[index]) : "0";
            string part2 = index < parts2.Length ? RemoveLeadingZeroes(parts2[index]) : "0";

            if (part1.Length < part2.Length) {
                return -1;
            }
            if (part1.Length > part2.Length) {
                return 1;
            }

            int comparison = string.CompareOrdinal(part1, part2);
            if (comparison < 0) {
                return -1;
            }
            if (comparison > 0) {
                return 1;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }

    private string RemoveLeadingZeroes(string part) {
        int firstNonZero = 0;

        while (firstNonZero < part.Length && part[firstNonZero] == '0') {
            firstNonZero++;
        }

        return firstNonZero == part.Length ? "0" : part.Substring(firstNonZero);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)` over the input characters.
Space complexity: `O(n + m)` for the split parts and temporary strings. Leading zeroes, zero-only parts, and missing revisions are covered.

